><year>2005</year><fpage>363</fpage><lpage>373</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1016/j.jtbi.2004.08.026">doi:10.1016/j.jtbi.2004.08.026</ext-link><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15572061</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib2"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Recker</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Pybus</surname><given-names>O.G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Nee</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gupta</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The generation of influenza outbreaks by a network of host immune responses against a limited set of antigenic types</article-title><source>Proc. Natl Acad. Sci. USA</source><volume>104</volume><year>2007</year><fpage>7711</fpage><lpage>7716</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1073/pnas.0702154104">doi:10.1073/pnas.0702154104</ext-link><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17460037</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib5"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>D.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Forrest</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hightower</surname><given-names>R.R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Perelson</surname><given-names>A.S.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Deriving shape-space parameters from immunological data for a model of cross-reactive memory</article-title><source>J. Theor. Biol</source><volume>189</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>141</fpage><lpage>150</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1006/jtbi.1997.0495">doi:10.1006/jtbi.1997.0495</ext-link><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9405131</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib6"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>D.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Forrest</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ackley</surname><given-names>D.H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Perelson</surname><given-names>A.S.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Variable efficacy of repeated annual influenza vaccination</article-title><source>Proc. Natl Acad. Sci. USA</source><volume>96</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>14&#x0200a;001</fpage><lpage>14&#x0200a;006</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1073/pnas.96.24.14001">doi:10.1073/pnas.96.24.14001</ext-link><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9874763</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>D.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lapedes</surname><given-names>A.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>de Jong</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bestebroer</surname><given-names>T.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Rimmelzwaan</surname><given-names>G.F.</given-names></name><name><surname>Osterhaus</surname><given-names>A.D.M.E.</given-names></name><name><surname>Fouchier</surname><given-names>R.A.M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mapping the antigenic and genetic evolution of influenza virus</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>305</volume><year>2004</year><fpage>371</fpage><lpage>375</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1126/science.1097211">doi:10.1126/science.1097211</ext-link><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15218094</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Bifurcation diagram plotting local maxima (taken from the last 5000 years of a 15&#x0200a;000 year solution of the model) of the proportion immune to each strain, <italic>Z</italic><sub><italic>i</italic></sub>, as a function of the cross-immunity parameter <italic>b</italic>. The plot illustrates the different types of dynamical behaviour of the model obtained as <italic>b</italic> varies. <italic>R</italic><sub>0</sub>=2 and the recovery rate <italic>&#x003c3;</italic>=50/year.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20080333f01"/></fig><fig id="fig2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Example of the functional form used to describe the decline in cross-immunity, <italic>&#x003b3;</italic>(<italic>d</italic>), with genetic distance, <italic>d</italic> (<italic>a</italic>=0.5, blue curve). <italic>a</italic>=0 corresponds to a flat function not changing with genetic distance (green curve).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20080333f02"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Dynamical maps for the models with (<italic>a</italic>) five locus three allele genotype and (<italic>b</italic>) eight locus two allele genotype. Parameter <italic>a</italic> (OX axis) characterizes decay of cross-immune response with genetic distance and <italic>b</italic> (OY axis) is the maximum cross-immunity level. In red areas, marked as NSS (no strain structure), dynamics converge to a point attractor with all strains present at constant prevalences. Light blue marks areas of CSS where quasi-periodic oscillations of prevalence occur. Dark blue indicates the small regions of pure periodic behaviour. Light green zones indicate DSS, where cross-immunity causes the system to self-organize into discordant (antigenically non-overlapping) sets of strains at constant prevalence.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20080333f03"/></fig><fig id="fig4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Bifurcation diagrams that characterize the model dynamics depending on the cross-immunity parameter <italic>a</italic>. Genotype consists of five loci and three alleles. <italic>R</italic><sub>0</sub>=2, <italic>&#x003c3;</italic>=50&#x0200a;yr<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>, and <italic>b</italic> is (<italic>a</italic>) <italic>b</italic>=0.5, (<italic>b</italic>) <italic>b</italic>=0.6, (<italic>c</italic>) <italic>b</italic>=0.7, (<italic>d</italic>) <italic>b</italic>=0.8.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20080333f04"/></fig><fig id="fig5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Strain infection prevalence for <italic>a</italic>=0.62 and <italic>b</italic>=0.6, taken from the region of long-period limit cycles <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig4">figure 4</xref><italic>b</italic>. <italic>R</italic><sub>0</sub>=2 and <italic>&#x003c3;</italic>=50/year.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20080333f05"/></fig><fig id="fig6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Time series of strain prevalence for example long-period solutions of the model for values of <italic>a</italic> above the Andronov&#x02013;Hopf bifurcation at <italic>a</italic>=0.03 (<italic>b</italic>=0.8, <italic>R</italic><sub>0</sub>=2, <italic>&#x003c3;</italic>=50/year): (<italic>a</italic>) <italic>a</italic>=0.038; (<italic>b</italic>) <italic>a</italic>=0.28; (<italic>c</italic>) <italic>a</italic>=0.44; (<italic>d</italic>) <italic>a</italic>=0.81.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20080333f06"/></fig><fig id="fig7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>(<italic>a</italic>) Dynamical map for model with no strain-transcending immunity, with an antigenic genotype of four loci and three alleles. Dynamical regimes labelled as <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig3">figure 3</xref><italic>a</italic>; (<italic>b</italic>) as (<italic>a</italic>) but showing average prevalence-weighted number of strains; (<italic>c</italic>) as (<italic>a</italic>) but for model with strain-transcending immunity, assuming <italic>&#x003b3;</italic>=3/year (i.e. four month duration of strain-transcending immunity); (<italic>d</italic>) as (<italic>c</italic>) but showing average prevalence-weighted number of strains.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20080333f07"/></fig><fig id="fig8" position="float"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Map showing mean period between peaks in infection incidence (reflecting the frequency with which new strains emerge) as a function of the cross-immunity parameters <italic>a</italic> and <italic>b</italic>, for models with four locus three allele antigenic genotype and (<italic>a</italic>) no strain-transcending immunity; (<italic>b</italic>) with strain-transcending immunity (other model parameters are the same as in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig7">figure 7</xref>). Colour scale shows period in years on logarithmic scale.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20080333f08"/></fig><fig id="fig9" position="float"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Sample time series of infection prevalence showing difference in dynamics and mean periods of epidemics for models (<italic>a</italic>) without strain-transcending immunity; (<italic>b</italic>) with strain-transcending immunity. Antigenic genotype consists of four loci and three alleles, cross-immunity parameters are <italic>a</italic>=0.7 and <italic>b</italic>=0.8 in both cases and other parameters as <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig7">figure 7</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20080333f09"/></fig><fig id="fig10" position="float"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Dependence of average prevalence-weighted strain diversity on the cross-immunity parameter <italic>b</italic> for (<italic>a</italic>) <italic>a</italic>=0 and no strain-transcending immunity, (<italic>b</italic>) <italic>a</italic>=0.6 and no strain-transcending immunity, (<italic>c</italic>) <italic>a</italic>=0 with strain-transcending immunity and (<italic>d</italic>) <italic>a</italic>=0.6 with strain-transcending immunity (other main model parameters are the same as in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig7">figure 7</xref>).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20080333f10"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>